  "summary": "Projecting lattice-screened wooden bay windows filtering light, air and privacy across the Islamic city.",
  "description": "The mashrabiya is a closed wooden oriel whose face is a lattice of hundreds of turned balusters, from coarse bottle shapes to mille-fieux fineness. Set over streets in Cairo, Damascus, Baghdad and beyond, it cooled interiors by drawing breezes across water jars, softened glare, granted household privacy while keeping street sightlines, and displayed status through lattice fineness. Modern architects revived the principle in kinetic and parametric sun-screens, from Jean Nouvel's Institut du Monde Arabe to contemporary Gulf facades.",
